 (is (= \\\\\\\"9\\\\\\\" (out \\\\\\\"<?php namespace N; function compact($a) { return 9; } echo compact('x');\\\\\\\"))))))\\\\n+\\\\n+\\\\n (deftest p9-compile-refusals-test\\\\n   (doseq [[source reason]\\\\n           [[\\\\\\\"<?php eval('$x = 1;');\\\\\\\" :eval]\\\\ndiff --git a/test/yang/php/antlr/programs_p9.cljc b/test/yang/php/antlr/programs_p9.cljc\\\\nindex 53cdffe3..c3970d96 100644\\\\n--- a/test/yang/php/antlr/programs_p9.cljc\\\\n+++ b/test/yang/php/antlr/programs_p9.cljc\\\\n@@ -90,10 +90,12 @@\\\\n        \\\\\\\"245\\\\\\\")\\\\n    (fx :extract-skip-and-prefix :dynamic-scope\\\\n        \\\\\\\"<?php $x = 1; extract(['x' => 4, 'y' => 5], EXTR_SKIP); extract(['z' => 6], EXTR_PREFIX_ALL, 'p_'); echo $x, $y, $p_z;\\\\\\\"\\\\n-       \\\\\\\"156\\\\\\\")\\\\n+       \\\\\\\"15\\\\\\\"\\\\n+       :diag [[2 \\\\\\\"Undefined variable $p_z\\\\\\\"]])\\\\n    (fx :compact-nested :dynamic-scope\\\\n        \\\\\\\"<?php $x = 3; $y = 4; $a = compact('x', ['y', 'missing']); echo implode(',', $a);\\\\\\\"\\\\n-       \\\\\\\"3,4\\\\\\\")\\\\n+       \\\\\\\"3,4\\\\\\\"\\\\n+       :diag [[2 \\\\\\\"compact(): Undefined variable $missing\\\\\\\"]])\\\\n    (fx :globals-element-write :dynamic-scope\\\\n        \\\\\\\"<?php $GLOBALS['x'] = 7; echo $x, $GLOBALS['x'];\\\\\\\"\\\\n        \\\\\\\"77\\\\\\\")])\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/orch-lane/1794720000000-architect-confirm-round.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:33:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_16\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The P1 diff removes the out-of-scope `fesc` reference and places the drain after the shutdown escape. The P8 manifest still has the three stale records, consistent with the remeasurement being pending. I’m checking the P9 edge cases and P1 fatal-bypass path before assigning verdicts.\\\"}}\\ncollab/orch-lane/1794720000000-architect-confirm-round.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:35:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_17\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/orch-lane/1794580000001-reviewer-retro-step7.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md; tail -180 collab/orch-lane/1794680000000-architect-consolidated-round.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"Created-Local: 2026-11-05 0x:xx +0700\\\\nCoding-Agent: codex (gpt-6.1-sol, architect step-7 retro review)\\\\nSession: read-only static review of the five session landings\\\\n\\\\nP7-a, P5, and P9 need forward fixes. The cross-language composition is acceptable as partial evidence: **its “no call rewriting” claim is true**. The ledger needs reconciliation.\\\\n\\\\nThis was static reading only; I ran no suites and changed no files. Examples below are predictions from the committed code, checked against the design and PHP documentation/source.\\\\n\\\\n**1. `6e587996` — P7-a budget: DEFECT**\\\\n\\\\n- **P1 — Exhaustion can execute guest code and lose the fatal.** [safepoint.cljc:20](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc:20) calls `php/unwind-to`, which executes `:finally` thunks. Those include guest `finally` blocks and local-variable cleanup that can invoke destructors. A `finally { return 1; }` can escape before `php/raise` delivers the operational fatal. This violates §12’s non-catchable fatal and destructor-skipping contract. Forward fix: use a terminal root escape that guest handlers, returns, and finalizers cannot replace.\\\\n\\\\n- **P1 — Exhaustion during shutdown takes the wrong result path.** [prelude.cljc:5092](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5092) recognizes an operational failure only in `exc0`. A shutdown callback exhausting its budget produces operational `exc2`, which goes through `snapshot-exc` as though it were a guest Throwable. Forward fix: preserve operational termination throughout body, exception-handler, and shutdown execution.\\\\n\\\\n- **P2 — Budget input is unchecked.** [safepoint.cljc:41](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/safepoint.cljc:41) accepts arbitrary values; a negative counter decrements forever without reaching zero. Reject values outside the documented non-negative integer domain.\\\\n\\\\nThe default-unlimited behavior, per-run cell, hook-only charging, and exact fatal text follow the intended design. The deterministic count is an explicitly documented PHP departure. This patch does not establish the signal-delivery portion of P7-a.\\\\n\\\\n**2. `453aa72e` — P5 request context: DEFECT**\\\\n\\\\n- **P1 — `exit` uses exception unwinding instead of escaping directly to the task root.** [prelude.cljc:445](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:445) calls `php/raise`. Consequently, `try { exit(7); } finally { echo 'F'; }` executes `F`, and a return from `finally` can cancel termination. Exit during shutdown also lacks proper root handling. PHP executes shutdown functions and destructors after exit, but skips `finally` blocks. Forward fix: install a distinct root exit continuation and preserve its status through shutdown. [PHP exit semantics](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.exit.php)\\\\n\\\\n- **P1 — Unclosed output buffers disappear.** [prelude.cljc:5082](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5082) reads only buffer zero; `php/shutdown!` never drains the remaining buffers. Thus `ob_start(); echo 'x';` loses `x`. This directly violates row 22 and C22, including destructor output inside buffers. Forward fix: flush nested buffers after the destructor phase, before releasing globals.\\\\n\\\\n- **P2 — Request binding is not integrated into the specified stream lifecycle.** The request injection occurs through a test-local postwalk at [e2e_harness.clj:160](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/php/antlr/e2e_harness.clj:160). Production initialization neither consumes a request nor seeds its superglobals; responses and diagnostics remain collected vectors rather than emissions on composition-supplied streams. The explicit binder is useful, but §3.4/§3.9 completion is not established. Forward fix: wire the actual request/response/diagnostics composition and exercise that path.\\\\n\\\\n- **P2 — `$_REQUEST` uses `array_merge` semantics.** [prelude.cljc:359](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:359) renumbers numeric keys and replaces nested arrays. PHP’s request merge preserves numeric keys and recursively merges nested arrays. GET-then-POST is a valid pinned order; its merge operation is wrong. Forward fix: implement the request-specific merge. [PHP 8.3 implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/main/php_variables.c)\\\\n\\\\n- **P2 — Accepted HTTP arguments lose semantics.** At [prelude.cljc:418](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:418), setting `http_response_code` returns `true`, rather than the previous status. `header` ignores its status argument; cookie records discard expiration, path, domain, secure, HttpOnly, and SameSite options. Forward fix: retain the supplied information and correct return values, or explicitly refuse unsupported forms. [Status semantics](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.http-response-code.php), [cookie arguments](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.setcookie.php)\\\\n\\\\n- **P1/P2 — Error triggering is incomplete.** [prelude.cljc:450](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:450) treats every level as a warning and continues, including unhandled `E_USER_ERROR`; invalid levels are accepted. The handler stack also retains only callbacks, leaving the inherited omission of handler masks unresolved. Forward fix: implement fatal user errors, validate levels, and save/restore callback plus mask. [Trigger errors](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.trigger-error.php), [handler masks](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.set-error-handler.php)\\\\n\\\\nThe explicit request data and task-local cells support the isolation design; I found no newly introduced ambient process reads or shared guest state.\\\\n\\\\nThe committed `:output-buffer-flush` fixture has an incorrect empty diagnostic expectation: its final `ob_end_clean()` must emit `E_NOTICE` because no buffer remains. [PHP 8.3 output implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/main/output.c)\\\\n\\\\n**3. `439ff42a` — P9 builtin surfaces: DEFECT**\\\\n\\\\nThere are defects beyond the disclosed `eval` and JSON-object/Unicode deferrals.\\\\n\\\\n- **P1 — Ordinary inputs can cause nontermination.** [prelude_p9.cljc:74](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:74) never advances for `str_ireplace('', 'x', 'a')`; [line 144](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:144) never progresses when padding is needed with an empty pad string; [line 358](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:358) loops forever for `range(1, 3, 0)`. These prelude loops have no source safepoints. Forward fix: handle empty searches and validate progress-making arguments before entering loops. PHP rejects zero range steps. [PHP range](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.range.php)\\\\n\\\\n- **P2 — String operations accept arguments they do not implement.** Examples: `str_replace` with an empty search returns an empty result; `str_pad('abcd', 2)` truncates; `strrpos` subtracts a positive offset from the backward starting position instead of enforcing the search’s lower bound. Character masks, replacement arrays/count output, and several optional arguments are silently ignored. See [prelude_p9.cljc:39](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:39), [line 84](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:84), and [line 145](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:145). Forward fix: implement each advertised signature or give a named refusal. [Padding semantics](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.str-pad.php), [reverse-search offsets](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.strrpos.php)\\\\n\\\\n- **P2 — Array operations do not preserve their contracts.** [prelude_p9.cljc:329](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:329) renumbers keys for single-array `array_map` and ignores additional arrays. `array_unique` uses loose equality instead of its default string comparison; diff/intersect ignore additional arrays. Splice bounds are not normalized, and omitted replacement becomes a vector value rather than an empty PHP replacement array. Forward fix: reconcile keys, comparisons, variadic inputs, and splice normalization with PHP. [array_map](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.array-map.php), [array_unique](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.array-unique.php), [array_splice](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.array-splice.php)\\\\n\\\\n- **P1/P2 — JSON can corrupt data and accept invalid syntax.** [prelude_p9.cljc:391](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:391) replaces unhandled control bytes with `?`. The parser accepts trailing commas, arbitrary escaped characters, and unchecked object separators. Associativity and depth are ignored; encoding error behavior does not respect `JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR`. Forward fix: preserve input data through correct escaping, enforce grammar/depth, propagate parse failure, and implement flags or refuse unsupported forms. Returning arrays for objects is disclosed in findings, but is still an unfinished parity obligation. [JSON encoding](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.json-encode.php), [decoding](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.json-decode.php)\\\\n\\\\n- **P2 — `extract`/`compact` semantics and constants are wrong.** [prelude_module.cljc:199](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_module.cljc:199) omits the mandatory underscore between prefix and key; existence tests use `isset`, incorrectly treating null-valued variables as absent; most extraction modes fall through to overwrite. [builtins.cljc:166](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/builtins.cljc:166) misnumbers `EXTR_PREFIX_INVALID` and `EXTR_PREFIX_IF_EXISTS` and introduces nonexistent constants. `compact` drops null-valued variables and silently skips missing names. Forward fix: correct constants, use binding existence, implement modes, and emit required warnings. [extract](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.extract.php), [compact](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.compact.php)\\\\n\\\\n- **P2 — New special cases bypass PHP name resolution.** [lower.cljc:1853](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:1853) intercepts `extract`/`compact` by fallback name even when a namespaced user function was resolved. Forward fix: apply builtin lowering only when the call actually resolves to that builtin.\\\\n\\\\n- **P2 — Math results fail outside the tiny fixtures.** [prelude_p9.cljc:539](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc:539) lets `abs(PHP_INT_MIN)` escape the PHP integer range. `round` ignores precision/mode; `min([3,1,2])` returns the array; the fixed 40-step square-root iteration is grossly inaccurate for large/small magnitudes. Forward fix: implement PHP’s numeric contracts and exceptional-value handling. [round](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.round.php), [min](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.min.php), [sqrt](https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.sqrt.php)\\\\n\\\\nThree committed fixtures specifically disagree with PHP 8.3:\\\\n\\\\n| Fixture | Required correction |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| `programs_p9.cljc:28`, multi-byte string assignment | Keep `aXZ`, but record `E_WARNING`: “Only the first byte will be assigned to the string offset.” |\\\\n| `programs_p9.cljc:91`, prefix `'p_'` | PHP creates `$p__z`; reading `$p_z` warns. Expected output is `15`, not `156`. |\\\\n| `programs_p9.cljc:94`, missing compact variable | Keep `3,4`, but record the missing-variable warning. |\\\\n\\\\nThe first-byte value change is correct; suppressing its warning is not. [PHP 8.3 assignment implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/php/php-src/PHP-8.3/Zend/zend_execute.c)\\\\n\\\\nThe named refusals for C6/C11/C12 and whole-`$GLOBALS` operations broadly follow the restriction table. They do not justify claiming the §15 P9 acceptance row—including caller-scope `eval`—complete.\\\\n\\\\n**4. `716326aa` + `a7ea0796` — cross-language matrix: ACCEPT, with notes**\\\\n\\\\nThe committed helper satisfies the narrow composition requirement:\\\\n\\\\n- It publishes real lowered donor bodies.\\\\n- It projects the donor’s callable value into the caller’s namespace.\\\\n- It neither replaces call nodes nor extracts foreign `:code`.\\\\n- Python’s injected namespace bindings are explicit composition, not language-pair call adapters.\\\\n\\\\nSee [conformance.clj:202](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/call/conformance.clj:202).\\\\n\\\\nThe evidence is narrower than the recorded “four-frontends conformance closed” claim:\\\\n\\\\n| Host | AST walker | Semantic | Stack | Register |\\\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\\\n| JVM | Explicitly excluded | Covered | Covered | Covered |\\\\n| Node / Dart | No evidence in this unit | — | — | — |\\\\n\\\\nThe tests cover the six directed pairs among TS, PHP, and Python. [cross_lang_test.clj:102](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj:102) is a **Clojure refusal assertion**, not an executed Clojure leg.\\\\n\\\\nThe matrix also does not establish all §8.10.11 gates: exception identity/rethrow, reference refusal and cleanup, suspension/context restoration, dynamic callability, custody, and the full binding/presence cases remain unproven here. Forward follow-up: record these limits explicitly and derive pair coverage from admitted profiles so new admissions cannot silently lack legs.\\\\n\\\\n**5. Ledger closures and pin changes: DEFECT in reconciliation; dedupe itself ACCEPT**\\\\n\\\\n- **Current master still contains the obsolete string-write refusal** at [known-gaps.md:107](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/agents/known-gaps.md:107), despite P9 implementing in-range writes.\\\\n- `bab43c51` updates that row and the stale pins, but the supplied refs place it on `feat/p8-legacy-retirement`, not master. Its single-byte outputs (`cb`, `xbc`) and `$$a[0]` output `1,20` agree with PHP 8.3. The latter follows PHP 7+ left-to-right variable syntax. [PHP syntax ruling](https://wiki.php.net/rfc/uniform_variable_syntax)\\\\n- At `bab43c51:test/yang/php/antlr/programs.cljc:574`, the fixture source changes while retaining the old dated oracle label. Forward fix: record the new source or mark its provenance pending.\\\\n- The string-write closure should explicitly retain C5’s remaining restrictions and the missing multi-byte warning; it must not imply unrestricted C5 parity.\\\\n- `d01a7c80` removes three identical datagram rows and retains one closed row citing `a226423d`. That dedupe loses no distinct ledger information. It is separate from these PHP/TS closures.\\\\n\\\\nThe correct ledger outcome is a narrowly closed **in-range single-byte write gap**, with the defects and unfinished acceptance obligations above retained explicitly.{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"thread.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"thread_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"01a1aa0e-c2be-7d61-98f4-d7dd0f2fdb2a\\\\\\\"}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"turn.started\\\\\\\"}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_0\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"I’ll read the architecture rules and the four worktree diffs, then issue the three verdicts with file-and-line evidence and landing authority. I’ll keep this pass read-only and leave behavioral validation to the recorded shiva gates.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pwd && rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g 'datom.world.md' -g SKILL.md docs .agents /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p1fix /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p5r /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p8 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cs 2>/dev/null\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pwd && rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g 'datom.world.md' -g SKILL.md docs .agents /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p1fix /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p5r /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p8 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cs 2>/dev/null\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld\\\\\\\\n.agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md\\\\\\\\ndocs/design/datom.world.md\\\\\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cs/AGENTS.md\\\\\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cs/docs/design/datom.world.md\\\\\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p5r/AGENTS.md\\\\\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p1fix/AGENTS.md\\\\\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p8/AGENTS.md\\\\\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p8/docs/design/datom.world.md\\\\\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p5r/docs/design/datom.world.md\\\\\\\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-p1fix/docs/design/datom.world.md\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"---\\\\\\\\nname: code-review-pr\\\\\\\\ndescription: >-\\\\\\\\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# Code Review PR Skill\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Overview\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\\\\\\\\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\\\\\\\\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\\\\\\\\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\\\\\\\\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Quick Start\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```bash\\\\\\\\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\\\\\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# 2. Review last commit\\\\\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\\\\\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# 4. Save review report to file\\\\\\\\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Code Review Workflow\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\\\\\\\\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\\\\\\\\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\\\\\\\\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\\\\\\\\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\\\\\\\\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\\\\\\\\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\\\\\\\\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\\\\\\\\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\\\\\\\\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\\\\\\\\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\\\\\\\\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\\\\\\\\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\\\\\\\\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\\\\\\\\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\\\\\\\\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\\\\\\\\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\\\\\\\\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\\\\\\\\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\\\\\\\\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\\\\\\\\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\\\\\\\\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\\\\\\\\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\\\\\\\\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\\\\\\\\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\\\\\\\\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\\\\\\\\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```markdown\\\\\\\\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Executive Summary\\\\\\\\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\\\\\\\\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\\\\\\\\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\\\\\\\\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\\\\\\\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\\\\\\\\n  ```diff\\\\\\\\n  - problematic code\\\\\\\\n  + suggested fix\\\\\\\\n  ```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\\\\\\\\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\\\\\\\\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\\\\\\\\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Utility Script Reference\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\\\\\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\\\\\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\\\\\\\\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\\\\\\\\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\\\\\\\\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Common Review Pitfalls\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\\\\\\\\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\\\\\\\\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_3\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_3\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"---\\\\\\\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Core Philosophy\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEverything is data.\\\\\\\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\\\\\\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\\\\\\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Foundational Axioms\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\\\\\\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\\\\\\\n   At system scale, a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\\\\\\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\\\\\\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\\\\\\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\\\\\\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\\\\\\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\\\\\\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\\\\\\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\\\\\\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\\\\\\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\\\\\\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\\\\\\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\\\\\\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Streams\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\\\\\\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\\\\\\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\\\\\\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\\\\\\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\\\\\\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\\\\\\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\\\\\\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\\\\\\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\\\\\\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\\\\\\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\\\\\\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\\\\\\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\\\\\\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\\\\\\\nthe stream's own.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\\\\\\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Host Boundaries\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\\\\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Architectural Commitments\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\\\\\\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\\\\\\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\\\\\\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Design Principles\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\\\\\\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\\\\\\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\\\\\\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\\\\\\\n  ordinary streams.\\\\\\\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\\\\\\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\\\\\\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\\\\\\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\\\\\\\n  auto-index observer.\\\\\\\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\\\\\\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\\\\\\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\\\\\\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\\\\\\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\\\\\\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\\\\\\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\\\\\\\nretired `:global` tag in\\\\\\\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\\\\\\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\\\\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Code Style\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\\\\\\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\\\\\\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\\\\\\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\\\\\\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Development Workflow\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\\\\\\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\\\\\\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\\\\\\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\\\\\\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\\\\\\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\\\\\\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\\\\\\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\\\\\\\n  leaves to review.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Problem Decomposition\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\\\\\\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\\\\\\\n2. Identify invariants.\\\\\\\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\\\\\\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\\\\\\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\\\\\\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\\\\\\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\\\\\\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\\\\\\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_4\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"I’m applying the repository’s `code-review-pr` skill, using your requested verdict format and static-only scope.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_5\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && git log -7 --oneline && git diff --stat master...HEAD && cat AGENTS.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_6\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && git log -7 --oneline && git diff --stat master...HEAD && cat AGENTS.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_7\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && git log -7 --oneline && git diff --stat master...HEAD && cat AGENTS.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_8\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && git log -7 --oneline && git diff --stat master...HEAD && cat AGENTS.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_9\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md && sed -n '1,150p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_9\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md && sed -n '1,150p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\\\\\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\\\\\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\\\\\\\\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\\\\\\\\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\\\\\\\\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### A. Logic & Correctness\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### B. Security & Credentials\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### C. Architecture & API Design\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\\\\\\\\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\\\\\\\\n2. **Explain the \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Why\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"**: Don't just say \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"change this\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\").\\\\\\\\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\\\\\\\\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nPR Reviewer Utility Script for Git-based Code Reviews.\\\\\\\\nParses git diffs, analyzes churn and file risk categories, scans for security smells\\\\\\\\nand debugging code, and produces structured markdown or JSON review reports.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nimport argparse\\\\\\\\nimport json\\\\\\\\nimport os\\\\\\\\nimport re\\\\\\\\nimport subprocess\\\\\\\\nimport sys\\\\\\\\nfrom dataclasses import asdict, dataclass, field\\\\\\\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\\\\\\\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n@dataclass\\\\\\\\nclass Finding:\\\\\\\\n    severity: str  # P0 (Critical), P1 (High), P2 (Medium), P3 (Low)\\\\\\\\n    category: str  # Security, Debug, Quality, ErrorHandling, TestCoverage\\\\\\\\n    file_path: str\\\\\\\\n    line_number: Optional[int]\\\\\\\\n    message: str\\\\\\\\n    snippet: str\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n@dataclass\\\\\\\\nclass FileStat:\\\\\\\\n    path: str\\\\\\\\n    additions: int\\\\\\\\n    deletions: int\\\\\\\\n    category: str\\\\\\\\n    risk_level: str  # High, Medium, Low\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n@dataclass\\\\\\\\nclass ReviewSummary:\\\\\\\\n    target: str\\\\\\\\n    total_files: int\\\\\\\\n    total_additions: int\\\\\\\\n    total_deletions: int\\\\\\\\n    test_additions: int\\\\\\\\n    source_additions: int\\\\\\\\n    test_to_source_ratio: float\\\\\\\\n    file_stats: List[FileStat] = field(default_factory=list)\\\\\\\\n    findings: List[Finding] = field(default_factory=list)\\\\\\\\n    commits: List[str] = field(default_factory=list)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\ndef run_git_command(args: List[str], cwd: Optional[str] = None) -> str:\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Helper to run git commands cleanly.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    cmd = [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"git\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"] + args\\\\\\\\n    try:\\\\\\\\n        res = subprocess.run(\\\\\\\\n            cmd,\\\\\\\\n            cwd=cwd,\\\\\\\\n            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\\\\\\\\n            stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\\\\\\\\n            text=True,\\\\\\\\n            check=True,\\\\\\\\n        )\\\\\\\\n        return res.stdout.strip()\\\\\\\\n    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:\\\\\\\\n        sys.stderr.write(f\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Git command failed: {' '.join(cmd)}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nError: {e.stderr}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\\n        raise RuntimeError(f\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Git execution failed: {e.stderr.strip()}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\ndef categorize_file(file_path: str) -> Tuple[str, str]:\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Categorizes file type and assigns risk level.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    path_lower = file_path.lower()\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    # Security & Auth\\\\\\\\n    if any(k in path_lower for k in [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"auth\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"secret\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"crypto\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"token\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"permission\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"jwt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"oauth\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"password\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]):\\\\\\\\n        return \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Security/Auth\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"High\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    # Database & Schema\\\\\\\\n    if any(k in path_lower for k in [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"migration\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"schema\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".sql\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"entity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"db/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]):\\\\\\\\n        return \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Database/Schema\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"High\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    # Infrastructure & Config\\\\\\\\n    if any(k in path_lower for k in [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"docker\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"terraform\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".tf\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".env\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"k8s\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"helm\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ci/cd\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".github/workflows\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]):\\\\\\\\n        return \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Infra/Config\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"High\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    if any(file_path.endswith(ext) for ext in [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"package.json\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"deps.edn\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Cargo.toml\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"go.mod\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"requirements.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pom.xml\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"build.gradle\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]):\\\\\\\\n        return \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Dependencies\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Medium\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    # API / Interfaces\\\\\\\\n    if any(k in path_lower for k in [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"proto\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"openapi\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"swagger\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"graphql\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"router\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"controller\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"api/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]):\\\\\\\\n        return \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"API/Contract\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Medium\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    # Tests\\\\\\\\n    if any(k in path_lower for k in [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"spec\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"mock\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fixture\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"e2e\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]):\\\\\\\\n        return \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Low\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    # Documentation\\\\\\\\n    if any(file_path.endswith(ext) for ext in [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".rst\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"LICENSE\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]):\\\\\\\\n        return \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Documentation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Low\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    # Core source\\\\\\\\n    return \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Source Code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Medium\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\ndef scan_diff_content(diff_text: str) -> List[Finding]:\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Scans diff text for added code smells and risks.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    findings: List[Finding] = []\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n    # Patterns to match added lines\\\\\\\\n    patterns = [\\\\\\\\n        # Secret leaks\\\\\\\\n        (\\\\\\\\n            r\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"(?i)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\+.*(api[_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\-]?key|secret[_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\-]?key|bearer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s+[a-z0-9_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\-\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.]{20,}|private[_\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\-]?key)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*[:=]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*['\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"][A-Za-z0-9%+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\/=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\-_]{8,}['\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"P0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Security\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Possible hardcoded credential or secret detected.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        ),\\\\\\\\n        (\\\\\\\\n            r\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\+.*-----BEGIN (RSA|OPENSSH|EC|PRIVATE) KEY-----\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"P0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Security\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Embedded private key detected.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        ),\\\\\\\\n        # Debug code left behind\\\\\\\\n        (\\\\\\\\n            r\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\+.*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\b(console\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.log|print\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\(|debugger;|binding\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.pry|import pdb; pdb\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.set_trace|println!|fmt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.Println)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"P3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Debug\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Leftover debug statement detected.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        ),\\\\\\\\n        # Dangerous functions\\\\\\\\n        (\\\\\\\\n            r\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\+.*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\b(eval\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\(|exec\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\(|os\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.system\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\(|subprocess\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.Popen\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\(.*shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*True|dangerouslySetInnerHTML)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"P1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Security\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Potentially unsafe dynamic execution or shell invocation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        ),\\\\\\\\n        # Swallowed exceptions\\\\\\\\n        (\\\\\\\\n            r\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\+.*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\b(catch\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\([^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\)]*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\}|except:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*pass|rescue\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*=>\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*nil)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"P2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ErrorHandling\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Swallowed exception without logging or handling.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        ),\\\\\\\\n        # Potential SQL Injection\\\\\\\\n        (\\\\\\\\n            r\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"(?i)^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\+.*((select|insert|update|delete)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s+.*%s|(select|insert|update|delete)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s+.*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\$[a-z0-9_]+|(select|insert|update|delete)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s+.*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\s*[a-z0-9_]+)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"P1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Security\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n            \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Potential SQL string concatenation detected. Use parameterized queries.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\n        ),\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_8\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short && git log -7 --oneline && git diff --stat master...HEAD && cat AGENTS.md'\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"git: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\\\\\\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-oVsLT7pX' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\\\\\\\n2026-11-05 11:54:37.395 xcodebuild[7527:110419764]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\\\\\\\n2026-11-05 11:54:38.087 xcodebuild[7527:110419752] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Input/output error\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\\\\\\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\\\\\\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-e1r9IVhQ' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\\\\\\\n2026-11-05 11:54:39.134 xcodebuild[7545:110419882]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\\\\\\\n2026-11-05 11:54:39.517 xcodebuild[7545:110419878] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Input/output error\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\\\\\\\n?? collab/orch-lane/1794590000000-engineer-cellref-sweep.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.findings.md\\\\\\\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path o