unting re-lists discharge\\u2019s answer set and may drift if discharge gains an obligation source | cmd 6d-a review caveat, acknowledged by Fable | semantic test harness | derive the answer set from discharge in a later harness slice | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:54:| JVM `dao.host.text/nfc` is Unicode 15.0 on the lane's JDK 21; 20 Unicode 16.0 canonical compositions (Todhri, Tulu-Tigalari, Gurung Khema, Kirat Rai) do not normalize; pinned by `text_test/jvm-nfc-gap` | H1 report F1; Fable review C1; owner acceptance amendment 2026-10-21 | host-boundary | open: owner decision on raising the JVM lane to JDK 24+ (recommended) | P2 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:59:| jolt J0c is probe-only: no lifecycle/close/reset, unavailable-helper behavior, or enforced input/nesting/time budgets; unary/indent stack limits are censored measurements, not safe bounds; cancellation unsupported; TSan not run and LSan has no positive control; the FFI cleanup cannot recover when no pointer is obtainable or the release primitive itself fails (injected frees that throw show release attempts, not reclamation); no production or frontend-readiness claim | astra J0c re-check 1792590000000; J0c r5/r6 reports | J5 integration seat (jolt) | closed 2026-10-27 in J5 (jolt-j5): the wrapper enforces a 4 MiB input bound (-6) and a 256-level nesting bound by a flat prescan (-7), clamped by the thread's stack, refused before any ANTLR object exists as `:parser-budget` (spec \\u00a77.6); the stage owns its worker (reset remakes it, close disposes it, closed workers refuse); a missing or wrong-layout library answers `:unavailable-parser` and a later selection retries; evidence `bb probe:jolt-j5` (below/at/above per axis, 10x the old crash depths in process, the old crash depths in child processes refusing, the fault-injection harness property), the J0c workers/cycles/sanitize legs, `bb test:jolt-j5`, both platforms (J5 findings `1793098786624`); the limits that survive are the next row. Platform qualification (owner waiver \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"linux only\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"): the corrected crash-depth driver, the -4 parity/sanitizer legs and the shell corpus of that closure ran on Linux x86_64 only (shiva, jupiter); the earlier macOS arm64 results predate those fixes. REOPENED 2026-10-28: the codex review of e4d43e5e found four prescan bypasses (a CR/form-feed-ended comment and backslash-space joins the prescan misread, uncounted comprehension `for` chains, unary runs discarded inside brackets, and flat binary chains whose deep trees the recursive walkers overflowed), and the Architect ruling replaced the prescan rather than repairing it (slice J5b). Closing evidence 2026-10-28 in J5b; the row closes with J5 when the codex re-review of J5b signs off (pending): the prescan is deleted; guards inside recognition refuse as status -7 `nesting <m> <b> rule|lookahead` before any buffer exists: 8,192 live rule frames (the frame depth is the length of the current context's parent chain, which equals the live rule activations; recorded per context at virtual rule entry), 4,096 SLL lookahead links and 256 full-LL links past the outer context (on every RULE transition of prediction); both walkers are iterative; recognition, export and worker disposal run on a wrapper-owned 16 MiB thread (spec \\u00a77.6). Evidence: `bb probe:jolt-j5` on macOS arm64 (the review's repros refusing three times then parsing `x * 6`; at/over per shape exactly at the predicted n; 10x the old crash depths; the old crash depths in children on the jolt thread; a 20,000-deep `1+` tree equal to the JVM's packet), `bb calibrate:jolt-j5` (every bound at most a quarter of the parse stack per shape, worst 1.6 MB), the lifecycle and accessor legs; Linux x86_64 (shiva 2026-10-29), all at the round-3b bytes: `bb probe:jolt-j5` rc 0, `bb test:jolt-j5` ALL PASS 0 FAIL, `bb calibrate:jolt-j5` rc 0 (worst quarter-stack margin unchanged), the J0c legs rc 0 (cycles 20,000 with 16-byte malloc growth, workers, parity zero pins), the sanitizer soak's 50 rounds all pass (its exit-time LeakSanitizer report is the classified P3 row below \\u2014 suppressed, the corpus reads HARNESS ok), and the leak experiment is cycle-independent (0/5/50/100 cycles all exactly 181,088 bytes in 3,312 allocations) | P2 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:60:| jolt Python parser limits surviving J5: no process crash isolation (an uncatchable native fault ends the jolt process); no worker-restart claim; no cancellation of an under-bound parse (time is bounded only through the input and nesting axes); TSan not run and LSan has no positive control; the FFI cleanup cannot recover when no pointer is obtainable or the release primitive itself fails; an above-bound source the JVM parses is refused on jolt (a recorded divergence, not a parity pin). J5b adds: the lookahead bounds (4,096 SLL links, 256 full-LL links past the outer context) refuse nesting the JVM parses (nested calls past 408 levels, bracket nests past 453 inside a call, unary runs past 253); time inside the bounds is not bounded by them: the pinned runtime parses a flat left-recursive chain in quadratic time (`1+1+...`: 16,000 operators 7.4 s, 100,000 about 340 s natively on macOS arm64; a 4 MiB chain hours) and an SLL decision whose lookahead walks a `*` chain grows faster (`1*1*...`: 2,000 operators 21 s, 4,000 about 490 s, refused at 4,097 links), so an under-bound source can hold the parse thread for minutes; the guards add up to about 2x on prediction-heavy shapes; a worker freed when no parse thread can be started is leaked, never destroyed on the caller's stack; the JVM oracle's own exporter overflows its default stack on a 20,000-deep tree (the comparison runs it with `-Xss1g`); a prediction whose context graph exceeds 4x the lookahead bound in distinct nodes refuses (a cap, not an undercount); measured, not gated: the backslash-joined chained assignment at 100,000 lines 89 to 150 s and the `1+` chain at 100,000 operators about 340 s (macOS arm64) | spec \\u00a77.6 | jolt seat | open; slice J5c named for the work budget (precondition for J6 Python peers) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:61:| jolt Python parser has no deterministic work budget: time inside the input and nesting axes is unbounded (a `1+` chain is quadratic in the pinned runtime; an SLL lookahead over a `1*` chain grows faster; a few-kilobyte source holds the parse thread for minutes), and no refusal is keyed on elapsed time (a refusal is a function of the source) | spec \\u00a77.6 | jolt seat | open; slice J5c: one `work` counter (every `getEpsilonTarget` call and rule entry), status -8 `work <measured> <bound>`, `{:diagnostic :parser-budget :axis :work}`, `kMaxWork` calibrated from the worst work per byte over the oracle corpus and the 4 MiB flat inputs; first show whether the counter sees the `1+` quadratic; acceptance: `1*`x4,000 and `1+`x100,000 refuse `:work` within 10 s on the laptop, the corpus and 4 MiB flat inputs parse unchanged, zero parity pins; precondition for parsing remotely sourced Python (J6) | P2 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:62:| Closed 2026-10-22: on jolt, `well-formed-utf16?` (`debruijn.cljc:253`, `debruijn_code.cljc:183`) bounded code-unit walk by `(count s)` (scalar count on Chez) causing astral strings to classify unsupported and under-counting UTF-8 byte length | glm Architect review of jolt-text-linear 1792646000000 (P2 follow-up, pre-existing in src/cljc) | yin.vm.debruijn owner with the host-boundary seat | fixed: walks bound by `dao.host.text/unit-count` across both files | closed |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:67:| VERIFIED: namespace `:doc` metadata is nil on jolt (`(:doc (meta (the-ns 'dao.stream.apply)))`, apply_test.cljc:227), causing NullPointerException in the vocabulary assertion | J2 rerun 2, reproduced on two bases | jolt seat / upstream jolt-lang | `:jolt nil` arm around the vocabulary assertion; track upstream namespace-metadata issue (not posted); JVM continues to inspe