imitives `first`, `rest`, `empty?`; the compile-time refusal covers local bindings only, so a store `(def first ...)` or a runtime initial-`:env` entry named `first`/`rest`/`empty?` captures identically through `engine/resolve-var` (env, then store, then primitives) and is invisible to it | architect session 8c1f0b7e, glm-5.3 review of yang-slice3 | yang.clojure | open: a module-qualified alias for the core primitives (a VM-side registration question) or a refusal of top-level definitions of the three names | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:122:| yang.clojure: a destructured `&` remainder is a vector (the VM `rest` contract), where Clojure yields a seq or list; consumers that observe the shape (`list?`, `seq?`-sensitive printing) differ | architect session 8c1f0b7e ratified departure, `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` `nthnext-form` | yang.clojure | slice 7 (Clojure-compatible sequence operations) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:123:| yang.clojure: variadic fn parameters (`[a & r]` at the top level of `fn` / `defn`) are rejected explicitly, also under the argument-vector convention | architect session 8c1f0b7e, corpus defines no variadic function | yang.clojure | slice 7 (the convention extends to variadic guest functions) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:124:| yang.clojure: under :arg-vector, a store-level `(def conj ...)`/`(def get ...)` inside one program unit captures the convention's bare conj/get operators (resolve-var puts store ahead of primitives); engineer-verified: `(defn conj ([] [9 9]))` makes `(g 1 2)` return [9 9]; the compile-time locals check does not cover defs; architect disposition 1793206800000: ledger row, closing route = refuse top-level def of conj/get/first/rest/empty? under the convention in slice 5; cross-unit case covered by the store-shadowing row above | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98, verified against engine.cljc:129-132); architect sign-off fable 8c1f0b7e | yang.clojure | slice 5 (convention code touched there) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:125:| yang.clojure: the ::defined scan in program-env reads only top-level heads, so a nested `(defn + ...)` inside do/if dispatches native while the store holds a convention closure \\u2014 silent wrong result on the supported surface; architect disposition: ledger row, closing route = refuse non-top-level def under the convention, added when slice 4 registers loop/recur as special forms | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98, traced example (+ [1 2]) -> [:pair 1 2] not :one) | yang.clojure | slice 4 | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:126:| yang.clojure: defmacro parameters are the one binder surface skipping check-binder!/reserved-prefix validation (consciously deferred: the (defmacro defn [& xs]) test needs &); a reserved-prefix macro param can be shadowed by a generated cursor; macros never enter the convention so impact is body-level only; architect disposition: ledger row, closing route = &-aware per-param binder check, slice 4 with the nested-def refusal | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98) | yang.clojure | slice 4 | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:127:| yang.clojure: value-position adapters for native primitives cap at 4 arguments (magic constant), so a variadic primitive like assoc ([3 :variadic]) is refused with :arity at 5+ args though the primitive supports it; loud, not silent; architect disposition: ledger row, closing route = derive admitted counts from :yin.k/arities profiles, slice 7 (guest library) | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98, verified against vm.cljc:376-421) | yang.clojure | slice 7 | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:128:| yang.clojure: reverse interop is latent \\u2014 a guest-convention fn value passed into a native/namespaced higher-order caller would be invoked with raw args (silent wrong on arity collision); no HOF primitive exists today; the corpus's one first-class use is (partial free-vars def-env); architect note: ledger now, closes when slice 5/7 apply/partial land | slice 3b review (glm session 79d4df98) | yang.clojure | slice 7 | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:129:| yang.clojure: three slice-4 residuals, bundled per architect disposition (fable 1793212600000): (1) a free REFERENCE to a reserved-prefix worker name (dsg__loop0 etc.) is not refused \\u2014 binder enforcement covers bindings only, so user-typed (dsg__loop0 dsg__loop0 3) inside a 2-binding loop reaches the worker and nil-fills; closing route: a compile-program pre-pass refusing reserved-prefix symbols in any position; (2) bare recur/loop symbols in VALUE position compile as ordinary variables (host Clojure rejects unbound recur; binder-shadowed case matches Clojure); closing route: same pre-pass refusing recur/loop outside operator position; (3) the wrong-operand-count rejection message says \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"loop bindings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" also for function-level targets (cosmetic; target records its kind) | slice 4 review (glm session 5ff7ec63, verified against compile-loop/clojure.cljc:879-955 and ast_walker.cljc zero :tail? references) | yang.clojure | slice 5 (all three: one pre-pass rides the outcome-channel work) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:130:| test-strategy: the test:routed docs gate checks working-tree-vs-index only, so a fully-staged or untracked docs change verifies zero bytes and reports green; closing route: diff against HEAD and print \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"unchecked (untracked)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" for paths absent from ls-files; regression probes to follow (TS-P3-01) | test-strategy review (glm session af452dd9, verified against affected.clj:648-656 changed-file sources) | dev.tooling | test-strategy-feedback-contract (QA/codex) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:131:| test-strategy: exit 0 conflates green-gate with JVM-feedback-only for :foundation dispatch, and an empty shard map passes vacuously (every? on [] is true); an automation wrapper cannot distinguish feedback-only from the full tri-host gate by exit code; closing route: distinct exit code or machine-readable verification-scope summary, explicit 0-namespace warning/refusal (TS-P3-02) | test-strategy review (glm session af452dd9, traced test_routed.clj:60-68 and quick_sharded.clj:107-116) | dev.tooling | test-strategy-feedback-contract; CLOSE BEFORE automated gate adoption | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:132:| test-strategy: build-n-test.md says \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the Node peer also ensures bb deps:npm\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\u2014 deps:npm is keyed on the Node REPL build prerequisite; \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"peer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" in this repo is the Dart exe (TS-P3-03) | test-strategy review (glm session af452dd9, verified against quick_sharded.clj:170 and bb.edn:197-213) | dev.tooling | test-strategy-feedback-contract | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:133:| yang.clojure: anonymous fn under the convention reports a nil name in its wrong-arity error data (lossy diagnostics only); closing route: carry a :yin.k/kind :anonymous marker plus the fn form's source metadata in the error data | slice 5 review (glm session in collab/1793242637826), architect disposition fable 8c1f0b7e | yang.clojure | slice 7 (first-class fns become common) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:134:| yang.clojure: the and/or if-chain expansions bind host gensym names (and__/or__) outside the dsg__ reserved-prefix regime that slice 5 hardens; a colliding user-typed and__<n> would shadow inside the expansion; closing route: reserved-prefix names bound through the internal let | slice 5 review (glm session in collab/1793242637826; pre-existing at a34a2251) | yang.clojure | slice 6 (same expansion family) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:135:| test-strategy: the throw-site table regeneration pokes a private test-ns function (throw-site-rows); only the failure message documents the procedure; closing route: a dev script beside src/dev/latte_oracle_manifest.clj that writes the table | slice 5 review (glm session in collab/1793242637826) | yang.clojure | slice 7 (next table-regenerating slice) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:136:| the ANTLR4 full-context prediction reach set leaks once per parser-budget refusal: the guard throws inside `ParserATNSimulator::closure`'s exception path, and the set is freed on the normal and no-viable-alt exits only; observed as a constant 181,088 bytes / 3,312 allocations on shiva at 0, 5, 50 and 100 sanitize cycles (cycle-independent); benign at the observed scale | wave-1 J5b closure sign-off | jolt seat | open: the fix (a ten-line reach-computation override in the bounded simulator) rides with J5c | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:137:| yang.clojure: slice-6 case-arm fixtures are by constant KIND, not per corpus site \\u2014 the plan's acceptance line asked for a minimal dispatch fixture for every arm of all six case sites (norm/proof keywords, mk-fresh numbers 0-3, typing symbols \\u03bb/\\u03a0); kinds are covered and mk-fresh runs end to end; closing route: derive fixtures from the corpus by walking the six sites, extracting each clause's constants, running a synthetic dispatch per arm+default (test-only addition) | slice 6 review (glm session 39b94aba), architect sign-off fable 8c1f0b7e | yang.clojure | slice 7 (test-only) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:138:| yang.clojure: no cljs/cljd lane evidence exists for the slice-6 commits yet (JVM + kondo only at sign-off time); the outcome-adapter reader-conditional catch remains the one host-sensitive line on the branch | slice 6 review (glm session 39b94aba, fifth item); architect condition: lanes green at 7677c12b before merge | yang.clojure | slice-6 landing gate (this merge) | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:139:| yang.clojure: library `reduced?` matches any map holding :yin.k/reduced true (untyped tagged map, documented compromise in the library notes); closing route: a typed carrier if the VM gains one | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | open | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:140:| yang.clojure: a `kind/*` or other qualified export in value position ignores its published arities (the adapter falls back to 0-4, so a bad count reaches the host); closing route: feed module profiles into the adapter counts alongside `:native` | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | slice 8 | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:141:| yang.clojure: library-internal closures report the compiler's own source positions (:line/:column of the quoted library forms) in anonymous-fn arity errors; closing route: strip :line/:column metadata when building `library` | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | slice 8 | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:142:| yang.clojure: `library-for` counts only `defn` as a program definition; a `def`/`defn-`/`defmacro` redefinition of a library name over-pulls the library definition (harmless, the program definition wins); closing route: share the compile-program defined-names scan | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | slice 8 | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:143:| yang.clojure: `apply` surface gaps against Clojure, e.g. `(apply :k [{:k 1}])` fails loudly where Clojure returns 1; closing route: lower a keyword or collection operator in apply to the static lookup | slice 7 review (glm), fix round | yang.clojure | open | P3 |\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:148:The original TS-P3 rows above remain unchanged. Fixes are in `a475d775`.\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:150:- **TS-P3-01: closed.** The docs route runs `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>`\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:157:- **TS-P3-02: closed.** Routing and quick-sharded print machine-readable EDN\\\\\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:168:- **TS-P3-03: closed.** `build-n-test.md` now names the Node REPL build\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793299000000-engineer-hardening2-fixround.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:3:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"I\\u2019ll read the repository rules, trace context propagation through the rebuild callers, and apply the P2 fix with focused regressions. Then I\\u2019ll run the requested checks and commit on `yang-hardening2` without pushing.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793294621496-compiler-engineer-hardening2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:6:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.started\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md docs/agents/format.md; cat test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md; cat deps.edn; rg -n 'slice9|gensym|free-vars|opacity|unfold-implicit|manifest|disposition' test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"in_progress\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ncollab/1793294621496-compiler-engineer-hardening2.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:7:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md docs/agents/format.md; cat test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_gate.md; cat deps.edn; rg -n 'slice9|gensym|free-vars|opacity|unfold-implicit|manifest|disposition' test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"---\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# BUILD & TEST\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Commands\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" below for\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" below).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` \\u2014 full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` \\u2014 documentation whitespace/format  |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` \\u2014 broad selected JVM        |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` \\u2014 existing quick tri-host         |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` \\u2014 fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\navailable. Do not run another lane set concurrently on the laptop.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  REPL.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# ClojureDart\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Linting\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"): a deftest that\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Remote test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nservers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" for how to run them):\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Run | Time |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|---|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nset -u\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${2:-8}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nSUBS=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n: > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$s\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\");print}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  cat \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndone\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfind test -type f \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\( -name \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"*_test.clj\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -o -name \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"*_test.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\) | sed -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#^test/##\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\$##\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#/#.#g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#_#-#g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | sort -u > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsort -u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncomm -23 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrun_shard() {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  s=$1; f=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; [ -s \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" ] || return 0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"); done < \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${args[@]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprintf \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"%s\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"run_shard {}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nset -u\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${2:-6}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrun_ns() {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nxargs -P \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"run_ns {}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" < \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand `git`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$URL\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\";\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ninto the clone:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Can't\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"One lane set at a time\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" are per-machine rules about\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"on one machine where\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\navoidable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"), not yet\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  verified by a measured run.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nseparate Dart clone.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### The full run\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nset -u\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDART=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${2:-}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nLOG=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$@\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n         echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nif [ -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  ( cd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfi\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwait\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  The assertion \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" gets no `bind-failed`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlaptop \\u2014 route Node lanes to the laptop or shiva); **Dart quick tier\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsame time \\u2014 they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Patch does not apply\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" or \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs \\u2014 `claude`, `codex`, `glm` \\u2014\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"run tests remotely\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nover SSH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\": a heavy implementation or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreference):\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Get the code there\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` after the `cd` \\u2014 the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   inside the same command.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   write its findings artifact \\u2014 the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   Workflow steps 6\\u20137 of the orchestrator role \\u2014 artifacts are verified, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 \\u2014 see Measured times;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first \\u2014 define what should happen.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   ```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   ```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   Common patterns:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndescription: File and content format rules for datom.world \\u2014 .chp/.blog EDN+Hiccup files, tables in .md files, and git commit message format\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# FORMATS\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`.chp` and `.blog` files are EDN files with Hiccup markup.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Structure for .blog files\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```clojure\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#:blog{:title \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Title Here\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n       :date #inst \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.000-00:00\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n       :abstract [:p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Abstract with hiccup markup...\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n       :content [:section.blog-article\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                 [:div.section-inner\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                  [:article\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                   :$blog-title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                   :$blog-date\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                   ;; Hiccup content here\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                   ]]]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Guidelines for .blog files\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Use EDN syntax with namespaced maps (`#:blog{...}`)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Use Hiccup vectors for all markup (`[:p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]`, `[:strong \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"bold\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]`, `[:a {:href \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"link\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]`)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Never use markdown syntax\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Include `:$blog-title` and `:$blog-date` where appropriate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Never use em dashes (\\u2014). Use commas, colons, periods, or parentheses instead.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Tables in .md files\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTables in `.md` files (design documents, agent guides) are ASCII box tables,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnever markdown pipe tables:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Rule              | Detail                                                                    |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+===================+===========================================================================+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Borders           | `+`, `-`, and `|` draw the box; a `+===+` rule separates the header row   |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|                   | from the body; every row closes with a full `+---+` border                |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Maximum width     | 170 columns, counted in characters (UTF-8 aware), including the borders   |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Column widths     | Columns that fit at their natural width stay unwrapped; wide columns      |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|                   | share the remaining budget proportionally to their content                |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Wrapping          | Long cell content word-wraps onto continuation lines that carry only the  |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|                   | wrapped column; other columns are blank on continuation lines; an         |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|                   | unbreakable token longer than its column hard-breaks at the column edge   |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe example table above is itself in the format: `docs/agents/team.md` and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` are the reference\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ninstances (the previous second instance, the v3 `yin.vm.semantic.md`, was\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndeleted at the 2026-10-15 cutover and its replacement carries no border\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntables).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Git commit messages\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is a repository-wide convention, not specific to any one role. Commit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsubjects use:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<type>[(<scope>)]: <lowercase imperative summary>\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`type` is one of `docs|feat|fix|refactor|perf|test|build|chore`. An optional\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbody explains non-obvious behavior or invariants; omit it when the subject\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nline is self-explanatory. Use no trailing period on the subject line. Merge\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncommits are exempt from this format. Never add `Co-Authored-By`, including\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nLLM attribution, to any commit in this repository.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nWhether and when a commit is *allowed* to happen (review requirements,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nauthorization, undoing an unauthorized commit) is role-specific policy, not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nformat \\u2014 see [`roles/orchestrator.md`](roles/orchestrator.md)'s Coordination\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncontract for the orchestrator's own gating rules.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n# Slice 9 kernel differential gate \\u2014 BLOCKED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is a failing close-out gate, not a conformance certificate. No compiler or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nVM source was changed. No commit was made.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe 54 fixtures in `kernel_gate.edn` invoke actual pinned upstream functions on A,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexecute the explicit context source on host Clojure on B, and compile the guest\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsource for C-walker and C-semantic. Guest extension closures are evaluated by the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nVM; the harness supplies no host callback primitive. The original proof dispatcher\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nis assembled, rather than substituting slice 8's restricted state-step dispatcher.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEvery fixture, including every negative, has an A/B/C-walker/C-semantic outcome in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`slice9_evidence.edn`. Blocked fixtures are attempted and recorded independently;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nno negative is skipped or counted as a successful guest rejection.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nResult: **4 passing fixtures, 2 mismatches, 48 blocked fixtures**. Passing fixtures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nare `:subst-capture`, `:subst-shadow`, `:subst-three-arity`, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`:unparser-guest-closure`. The compile/admission blockers occur before either C\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlane executes those blocked programs; both lane records explicitly say so.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Mismatches with minimal repros\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. `:minimal-gensym-freshness`:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   ```clojure\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   (let [a (gensym \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fresh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") b (gensym \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fresh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")] (= a b))\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   ```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   A and B return `false`; both C lanes return `[:yin.k/ok true]`. The guest\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   library's one-argument `gensym` constructs `G__fresh` repeatedly. This\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   dependency is reachable from `latte-kernel.typing/rebuild-type`. No fresh-name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   correspondence can turn equality of two names into inequality.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. `:free-vars-local-capture`:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   ```clojure\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   (latte-kernel.syntax/free-vars\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n     (latte-kernel.defenv/register-definition env\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n       (latte-kernel.defenv/->Definition 'd [] 0 'x '#{x} 'A {}) true)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n     '(d))\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   ```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   A returns `#{x}`; B returns `#{}`; both C lanes return `[:yin.k/ok #{}]`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   The upstream `free-vars` implementation uses `(second def-env)` to access\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   local definitions. The inherited context adaptation passes a context map in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   that position. This is an A/B adaptation mismatch, independently of compilation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Blocked dependencies\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Parsing reaches `butlast`, `last`, bare `nth`, and `kind/var?`, which are not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  admitted by the supplied portable modules. Minimal independent fixtures for\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `last`, `nth`, and `var?` are included. The parser's valid and malformed cases\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  all run on A/B, but their guest dependency closures fail admission.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Normalization reaches `last` in `nbe/evaluation` and `with-meta` in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `nbe/quotation-`. `:minimal-last` and `:minimal-with-meta` isolate these failures.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  Beta, transparent delta, opaque delta, and each required public normalization\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  arity have named fixtures; no host normalization is substituted for C.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Typing reaches the actual `typing/unfold-implicit` `try/catch`, including\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `.getMessage`, `ex-data`, and exception bindings. Slice 8's already-typed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  extension adapter does not replace this full typed-argument path. The two\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  implicit fixtures use actual guest closure source and remain blocked.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `typing/type-of-ascribe` is declared but has no definition in the pinned\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  upstream source and no manifest entry. `:typing-ascription` also exposes the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  upstream unbound-function failure on A. It is never treated as a successful\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  type rejection by C.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Full proof elaboration/checking reaches `proof/+opacity-fn+`, an ambient atom\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  initialized with a closure. Yang rejects this `def` initializer under the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  outcome convention. Each affected fixture records\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `:blocked-by [gate-proof-+opacity-fn+]` and the exact `:minimal-source`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  The inherited partial proof-step context adapter cannot stand in for the real\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  parsing/typing dispatcher. Proof compilation separately reaches the missing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `string?` dependency; even `:compile-proof`'s single `:qed` script is explicitly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  blocked by this dependency rather than treated as a successful rejection.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Upstream's error for steps after `qed` includes the host `clojure.core/meta`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  function in its `:meta` field. Evidence explicitly marks this nonportable host\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  value; it is not normalized into an accepted portability difference. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  malformed scalar proof-step fixture also records its native upstream exception.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe complete source dependency graph, per-fixture closure membership, missing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsymbols and their owning definitions, compiler exception causes, and minimal\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncalls are retained in `slice9_evidence.edn`. Compiler/admission/VM exceptions are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nblockers; only actual returned `:ko`, `false`, or corresponding domain exceptions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncan satisfy negative fixtures. A test assertion checks that every completed A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnegative really rejects its input.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Manifest and coverage\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe inherited manifest contains **155 bindings, 147 functions, and 175\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfunction/arity entries**,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot the plan's earlier estimate of 143 definitions. It has no admitted/excluded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndispositions, and its source fields are nil. Consequently an exact admitted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfunction/arity denominator cannot be recovered honestly from that manifest.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`admitted-function-arity-count` is explicitly `:not-recorded-in-inherited-manifest`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAll 175 entries are retained as gate obligations; successful fixtures do not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnarrow the denominator.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe conservative, proven direct A/B/C agreement count is **2/175 arities**:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`latte-kernel.syntax/subst` at arities 2 and 3. The successful unparser integration\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nalso executes its dependencies, but this harness does not claim an exact dynamic\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntransitive arity count. It records `:transitive-execution-coverage :unproven`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAll **173 remaining unexercised-or-unproven entries** are individually listed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nunder `:coverage :unexercised`, alongside the complete function/arity inventory.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDependency closure membership is recorded separately and does not count as\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexecution. This incomplete accounting itself blocks the close-out gate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Reproduction and verification\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nPins: upstream LaTTe `86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279`; compiler/VM\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`f74f2cecccfc54813671555b15e39d254debd2d9`; context adaptation v1, kernel assembly v1.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe recorded host uses Clojure 1.12.4 and Java 17.0.18. Each C execution has a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1,000,000-instruction budget. There is no continuation-depth claim for blocked\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprograms.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRun the gate:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -M:test -n yang.latte-oracle-test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**22 tests, 988 assertions, 1 failure, 0 errors, exit 1.** The single failed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nassertion is `test-kernel-differential-gate`: the gate must remain red while the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nabove blockers or unproven coverage remain. The inherited 21 tests passed in the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbaseline: 968 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. Do not remove the close-out\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nassertion to manufacture the requested zero-failure result.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nExplicitly refresh evidence from fresh A/B/C runs (normal test runs do not rewrite\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nit):\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -Sdeps '{:aliases {:gate {:main-opts [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"-e\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"(require (quote yang.latte-oracle-test)) (println (select-keys (yang.latte-oracle-test/write-kernel-gate-evidence!) [:gate-status :counts :fixture-count :manifest-bindings :function-arity-candidates]))\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:test:gate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis evidence-writing command returns normally even when the recorded gate is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nblocked; only the test command above is the acceptance gate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nUnchanged regression suites:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -M:test -n yang.clojure-test -n yin.vm.macro-test -n yang.clojure.stream-eval-test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Blocked at namespace loading**, `ClassNotFoundException:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nyang.python.antlr.gen.Python3Lexer`; no green four-suite result. The stream/REPL\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsuite imports the frontend catalog that requires the generated Python parser.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe prerequisite attempt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`clj -M:antlr-gen --grammar python3 --target java` failed with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`java.nio.channels.UnresolvedAddressException` while retrieving required inputs.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe independent suites were then run separately:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -M:test -n yang.clojure-test -n yin.vm.macro-test -n yin.vm.store-write-audit-test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclj -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic.linearize-test -n yin.vm.semantic.analysis-test -n yin.vm.semantic.frame-test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRespectively **106 tests / 1,007 assertions** and **45 tests / 1,944 assertions**,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nboth with **0 failures and 0 errors**.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nCross-host regression availability is separate from the JVM kernel oracle:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `bb test:changed:cljs --changed test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj test/yang/latte_oracle/kernel_gate.edn`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  could not start: `bb` is absent (exit 127).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Fallback `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test` failed before any\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  Node tests ran. `CompilerOptions` requires class-file version 65 (Java 21), but\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  the active runtime supports version 61 (Java 17). Node itself is available.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Dart/Flutter are absent from PATH. No Dart suite ran; that lane is unavailable,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  not green. Four-evaluator stream/REPL verification remains blocked as above.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `git diff --check` passed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n{:deps {org.clojure/core.async {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.8.741\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        org.clojure/tools.reader {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.5.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ring/ring {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.14.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        com.cognitect/transit-clj {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.0.333\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        org.replikativ/boring {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"0.1.30\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        com.datomic/peer {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.0.7364\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        org.postgresql/postgresql {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"42.7.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        org.clojure/data.json {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2.5.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2.8.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        org.jsoup/jsoup {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.18.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        instaparse/instaparse {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.5.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ;; yang.python.antlr: the runtime the generated Python3 parser runs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ;; on; pinned to the tool version in :antlr-gen (they are one release)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        org.antlr/antlr4-runtime {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"4.13.2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        criterium/criterium {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"0.4.6\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3 {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        stigmergy/chp {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"0.1.0-SNAPSHOT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                       :exclusions [refactor-nrepl/refactor-nrepl]}}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n :cljd/opts {}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n ;; build/antlr/python3/classes: the generated Python3 parser, produced by\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n ;; `clj -M:antlr-gen` (antlr/python3/manifest.edn); absent until generated\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n :paths [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/clj\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/cljs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/cljd\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n         \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"build/antlr/python3/classes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"build/antlr/php/classes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n         \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"build/antlr/typescript/classes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n :aliases {:clean {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/dev\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                   :exec-fn clean/clean}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n           :profile-btree {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/dev\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                           :extra-deps {persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                                        {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"0.3.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                           :main-opts [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"-m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"btree-bench\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n           :test {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                  :extra-deps {io.github.cognitect-labs/test-runner\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                               {:git/tag \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"v0.5.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" :git/sha \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"dfb30dd\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                               ;; latte-kernel: the LaTTe proof assistant's kernel,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                               ;; the differential oracle for the yang.clojure gap\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                               ;; program (pinned; MIT; dependency-free)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                               latte-kernel/latte-kernel\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                               {:git/url \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"https://github.com/latte-central/latte-kernel\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                                :git/sha \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                  :main-opts [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"-m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cognitect.test-runner\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                  :exec-fn cognitect.test-runner.api/test}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n           :nREPL {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                   :extra-deps {nrepl/nrepl {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"1.0.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                                cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"0.58.0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                   :main-opts [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"-m\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nrepl.cmdline\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"--middleware\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"[cider.nrepl/cider-middleware]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n           :cljs {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"src/dev\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"build/antlr/python3/js\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                  :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"3.3.5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                               reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"2.0.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\